            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
            are the left and right
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitor"} unitors
          %h2#morphism_of_monoids Morphism of monoids
          %p
            The analogue of a monoid homomorphism, called a morphism of monoids, is a morphism,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} f
                  %mo :
                  %mi M
                  %mo →
                  %mi M
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \f: M \to M'
            between two monoid objects, satisfying the equations;
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi μ
                  %mo =
                  %mi μ
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \circ \mu = \mu' \circ (f \otimes f)
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi η
                  %mo =
                  %mi η
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \circ \eta = \eta'
          %p corresponding to the commutative diagrams;
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
